# Your first card

This tutorial builds a `DataCard` from a pandas DataFrame, writes it to disk, and
reads it back. It is the exact example that ships in the repo, explained step by
step. No server is involved: `save` and `load` operate on the local filesystem
only.

<Aside title="Before you start" data-variant="wyrd">

Set up the repo first — see [Get started](/get-started/). You can run the whole
thing with `mise run examples:python:datacard`; this page explains what it does.

</Aside>

## The full example

<CodeFromFile file="examples/python/datacard_local_workflow.py" lang="python" />

Run it:

```bash
mise run examples:python:datacard
```

```text
saved_card_json: true
saved_data_file: data/data.parquet
loaded_rows: 3
loaded_columns: customer_id,churned,segment
labels: domain=customer,stage=example
annotation_source: examples/python/datacard_local_workflow.py
```

## Step by step

### 1. Build the card

```python
card = DataCard(
    PandasInterface(data=data),
    name="customer-churn",
    version="0.1.0",
    labels={"domain": "customer", "stage": "example"},
    annotations={"example.com/source": "examples/python/datacard_local_workflow.py"},
)
```

The first positional argument is a **data interface** — `PandasInterface` owns the
local save/load behavior for a DataFrame. `name` and `version` set the card's
identity; `space` defaults to `default`, so this card's identity is
`(default, customer-churn, 0.1.0)`.

`labels` are indexed classification fields you filter on. `annotations` are
descriptive metadata that travels with the card but is not indexed. Both are part
of the card envelope — see [Card](/concepts/card/).

### 2. Save to disk

```python
card.save(path)
```

`save` takes a directory path and writes the local card layout there:
`card.json` (the envelope) plus a `data/` directory holding the serialized
DataFrame (`data.parquet`). Nothing leaves your machine.

### 3. Load it back

```python
restored = DataCard.model_validate_json((path / "card.json").read_text())
restored.load(path)
```

`model_validate_json` reconstructs the card envelope from `card.json`. At that
point the metadata is populated but the data is not — `load(path)` reads the
DataFrame back from the `data/` directory into `restored.data`.

### 4. Read what you saved

```python
restored.data          # the DataFrame, 3 rows
restored.labels        # {"domain": "customer", "stage": "example"}
restored.annotations   # {"example.com/source": "..."}
```

The restored card carries the same identity, labels, annotations, and data the
original declared.

## What ships and what doesn't

<Aside title="Registration is not on the Python surface yet" data-variant="not">

`DataCard.save` and `.load` are local-only by design; the card object does not
talk to a server. Registering a card to a registry — so it gets a durable
server-side identity and joins the lineage graph — belongs to the registry and
client APIs, which are not yet exposed in the Python package. This tutorial
covers the shipped local workflow.

</Aside>

